            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Writes a tensor to a file in PyTorch's own format, exactly as it is: every value, the shape, the number type. A dict of tensors (a set of recordings, a model's weights, a calibration) or a list of them is written as one file. t.load reads it back, and so does torch.load in any Python script.\n\nA tensor on the GPU is copied to the cpu as it is written, so the file opens on any machine. A list of numbers, or of rows of numbers, is written as one tensor. Without an extension, .pt is added.\n\nWRITING ONLY WHEN ASKED:\nWhat arrives at in is only held, not written: a stream of tensors does not rewrite the file sixty times a second. The file is written when write is clicked (or sent a bang).\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.save <path>\nDefault: no path - the first write asks where to save.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.save recordings/take_1.pt\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe tensor, list or dict to save. Held until written; each new one replaces the last.\n\nwrite (button, also an inlet):\nWrites what is held to the path. With no path yet, it asks where, as save as does.\n\nsave as (button, also an inlet):\nAsks for a file name and folder, writes there, and keeps that path for the next write.\n\npath:\nAn option, under the node: where write saves. A path that is not absolute is relative to the folder the app was started from. It is saved with the patch.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsaved path:\nThe full path of the file, sent each time it is written - ready to pass on, or to show where the file went.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFour values arrive and are held. Click write (on the node) and choose where to save;\nthe path appears below. Nothing is saved until you do, and nothing is saved in the help folder.\n\nRELATED:\nt.load reads the file back. np.save writes numpy's format, for numpy and other programs.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
